          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My son has been wheezing at night for a month and his blue inhaler is running out fast. Should he be seen urgently, and what does asthma guidance describe about stepping up treatment?",
          "answer": "**Urgency:** Based on the description, the red-flag check found no emergency pattern, but that is not a clearance. Serious symptoms can fall outside the tool's list. A reliever inhaler running out fast suggests his asthma may not be well controlled, so book a doctor's review soon rather than waiting for a routine slot. The sources don't give a specific timeframe. (red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty; condition-asthma)\n\n**Get emergency help immediately if your son has any of these:**\n- Breathlessness at rest, or breathlessness that comes on suddenly\n- Trouble speaking in full sentences, or pausing for breath between words\n- Blue or grey lips, tongue, face or fingertips\n- Drowsiness, confusion, agitation or exhaustion\n- Gasping, or a high-pitched noise when breathing in\n- Fast breathing, skin sucking in between or under the ribs, grunting, or flaring nostrils\n- Too breathless to feed or talk\n- No improvement after reliever treatment, or symptoms coming back within a few hours\n- A chest that has gone quiet. In a severe attack, a lack of wheezing is a serious sign, not a reassuring one.\n\n(red-flag-severe-breathing-difficulty; condition-asthma)\n\n**Stepping up treatment:** Asthma guidance describes a stepwise approach. The step is chosen from current symptom control and past flare-ups, and it is reviewed rather than fixed. Lower steps use as-needed or regular low-dose preventer inhalers. Higher steps add stronger or combination treatment, and eventually referral to a specialist. Before any step-up, clinicians should check inhaler technique and whether the medicine is being taken as prescribed, since these account for a large share of apparent treatment failure. (guideline-asthma-diagnosis-and-stepwise-therapy)\n\n**Questions for the appointment:**\n- Can the clinician check how your son uses his inhalers, including whether he uses a spacer?\n- Is his preventer inhaler being taken every day?\n- Can you have a written asthma action plan?\n- What would the next treatment step be, and how will his progress be reviewed?\n\n(lifestyle-asthma-adherence; condition-asthma)\n\n**Limits:** The sources do not say whether your son needs a change in medicine. That decision belongs to his clinician, who can examine him. Do not change or stop any inhaler without advice. The passage on asthma guidance does not say how strong the evidence is for each step, so I cannot grade it. I also could not find where authorities disagree on this point, so I cannot tell you whether they differ.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
